Z||000000||four50four||07-04-99||02:04 PM||||please could anyone send me info on there 88 gt? also whats the pricing gonna be on one of these machines? thanx
Z||000001||Eric||07-04-99||03:04 PM||||I've had my 88GT for 5 months now and like it more everyday, mainly because I hear of the effort 84-87 owners put into their cars to make them handle as good as an 88.  I liked the looks of the later GT models, so I set out to purchase one in good condition and not too many miles.  I found mine in Texas (I live in Ohio) for around $4000.  From what I've seen, you can expect to pay between $3000 and $6000 for an 88GT in good condition with average miles.  That's more than a similar 87GT, but is well worth the money if you intend to drive the car the way it was meant to.  <p>Are you planning on throwing a big block in the back?  That may be cool, but I think the weight would comprimise the handling of the car.  A lot of people put small blocks in the 88s.  They provide increased power and torque without adding too much weight.  My choice is a 3.1 rebuild.  Using the stock engine, I can put a crankshaft out of a 3.1 motor with 3.1 pistons and get about 200 horsepower.  It doesn't add any weight, so I won't need to beef up the suspension or do any mods. to the cradle.<p>The suspension, I keep refering to, is a complete redesign only on the 88 model year.  For racing it is the best way to go.  It is also the best for the street, although I may get some debate about this.  <br>
Z||000002||87GT||07-04-99||04:03 PM||||87GT's that I always see are in that very same price range.  
Z||000003||Pretender||07-04-99||07:37 PM||||As it is here., it seems as if the only people who know that the suspension is better are everone but the dealers. i bought my 88gt for less then the 87 that was on the lot, only difference was that it had different rims on it, but i really lucked out when i got it because i had no idea that the 88's were any different, i just thought it looked nice...:o)
